    "problem": "The sum of the first $n$ terms of a sequence is given by $S_n = \\frac{a(1-r^n)}{1-r}$, where $a$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio. Let $S_{2011} = 200$ and $S_{4022} = 380$.\n\nWe can write the following equations:\n\n$$\\frac{a(1-r^{2011})}{1-r} = 200 \\tag{1}$$\n$$\\frac{a(1-r^{4022})}{1-r} = 380 \\tag{2}$$\n\nWe can simplify equation (1) by multiplying both sides by $(1-r)$:\n\n$$a(1-r^{2011}) = 200(1-r)$$\n\nNow, we can substitute this expression for $a(1-r^{2011})$ into equation (2):\n\n$$\\frac{200(1-r)(1-r^{2011})}{1-r} = 380$$\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n$$200(1-r^{2011}) = 380(1-r)$$\n\nNow, we can solve for $r$:\n\n$$1-r^{2011} = \\frac{380}{200}(1-r)$$\n\n$$1-r^{2011} = \\frac{19}{10}(1-r)$$\n\n$$10-10r^{2011} = 19-19r$$\n\n$$10r^{2011} - 19r = -9$$\n\n$$r(10r^{2010} - 19) = -9$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be negative (otherwise, the sequence would not converge), we know that $10r^{2010} - 19 > 0$, so:\n\n$$r = \\frac{-9}{10r^{2010} - 19}$$\n\nNow, we can use this expression for $r$ to find the sum of the first 6033 terms:\n\n$$S_{6033} = \\frac{a(1-r^{6033})}{1-r}$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $r$, we get:\n\n$$S_{6033} = \\frac{a(1-(\\frac{-9}{10r^{2010} - 19})^{6033})}{1-\\frac{-9}{10r^{2010} - 19}}$$\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n$$S_{6033} = \\frac{a(10r^{2010} - 19)^{6033} + 9^{6033}}{(10r^{2010} - 19)^{6033}}$$\n\nNow, we can use the fact that $S_{2011} = 200$ to find $a$:\n\n$$200 = \\frac{a(1-r^{2011})}{1-r}$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $r$, we get:\n\n$$200 = \\frac{a(10r^{2010} - 19)}{10r^{2010} - 19 + 9}$$\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n$$200 = \\frac{a(10r^{2010} - 19)}{10r^{2010} - 10}$$\n\nNow, we can solve for $a$:\n\n$$a = \\frac{200(10r^{2010} - 10)}{10r^{2010} - 19}$$\n\nSubstituting this expression for $a$ into the expression for $S_{6033}$, we get:\n\n$$S_{6033} = \\frac{\\frac{200(10r^{2010} - 10)}{10r^{2010} - 19}((10r^{2010} - 19)^{6033} + 9^{6033})}{(10r^{2010} - 19)^{6033}}$$\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n$$S_{6033} = 200 + \\frac{1800(10r^{2010} - 19)^{6032}}{(10r^{2010} - 19)^{6033}}$$\n\nNow, we can use the fact that $S_{4022} = 380$ to find $r$:\n\n$$380 = \\frac{a(1-r^{4022})}{1-r}$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $a$, we get:\n\n$$380 = \\frac{\\frac{200(10r^{2010} - 10)}{10r^{2010} - 19}(1-r^{4022})}{1-r}$$\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n$$380 = \\frac{200(10r^{2010} - 10)(1-r^{4022})}{(10r^{2010} - 19)(1-r)}$$\n\nNow, we can solve for $r$:\n\n$$1-r^{4022} = \\frac{380}{200}\\frac{(10r^{2010} - 19)(1-r)}{10r^{2010} - 10}$$\n\n$$1-r^{4022} = \\frac{19}{10}\\frac{(10r^{2010} - 19)(1-r)}{10r^{2010} - 10}$$\n\n$$10r^{4022} - 10 = 19(10r^{2010} - 19)(1-r)$$\n\n$$10r^{4022} - 10 = 190r^{2010} - 361 - 190r^{2011} + 361r$$\n\n$$10r^{4022} - 190r^{2011} + 190r^{2010} - 361r = -371$$\n\n$$r(10r^{4021} - 190r^{2010} + 190r^{2009} - 361) = -371$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be negative (otherwise, the sequence would not converge), we know that $10r^{4021} - 190r^{2010} + 190r^{2009} - 361 > 0$, so:\n\n$$r = \\frac{-371}{10r^{4021} - 190r^{2010} + 190r^{2009} - 361}$$\n\nNow, we can use this expression for $r$ to find the sum of the first 6033 terms:\n\n$$S_{6033} = 200 + \\frac{1800(10r^{2010} - 19)^{6032}}{(10r^{2010} - 19)^{6033}}$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $r$, we get:\n\n$$S_{6033} = 200 + \\frac{1800(10r^{2010} - 19)^{6032}}{(10r^{2010} - 19)^{6033}}$$\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n$$S_{6033} = 200 + 1800(\\frac{-371}{10r^{4021} - 190r^{2010} + 190r^{2009} - 361})^{6032}$$\n\n$$S_{6033} = 200 + 1800(\\frac{-371}{10r^{4021} - 190r^{2010} + 190r^{2009} - 361})^{6032}$$\n\n$$S_{6033} = 200 + \\frac{1800(-371)^{6032}}{(10r^{4021} - 190r^{2010} + 190r^{2009} - 361)^{6032}}$$\n\nNow, we can use the fact that $S_{2011} = 200$ to find $r$:\n\n$$200 = \\frac{a(1-r^{2011})}{1-r}$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $a$, we get:\n\n$$200 = \\frac{\\frac{200(10r^{2010} - 10)}{10r^{2010} - 19}(1-r^{2011})}{1-r}$$\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n$$200 = \\frac{200(10r^{2010} - 10)(1-